   "stem": {
      "text": "Let s be defined by s(x) = k sin(x) for x < pi/2, s(x) = cos(x) + 5 for pi/2 <= x <= pi, and s(x) = kx/pi + 1 for x > pi, where k is a constant. For what value of k is s continuous at x = pi/2?"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 5
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Just to the left of x = pi/2 the rule is k sin(x), so the left-hand limit is k sin(pi/2) = k.",
         "mathjson": "k"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At x = pi/2 and to its right the rule is cos(x) + 5, so the right-hand limit and s(pi/2) are cos(pi/2) + 5 = 5.",
         "mathjson": 5
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Continuity at x = pi/2 needs k = 5.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            "k",
            5
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So k = 5.",
         "mathjson": 5
      }
   ],
